```c
ret = snprintf(dst, rest(dst, end), "\"%s\"%s", val, vnode->next ? ", " : "\n");
```
```c
ret = snprintf(dst, rest(dst, end), "\"%s\"\n", val);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the code incorrectly uses double quotes to enclose boot configuration values, even when those values contain double quote characters. This can lead to parsing errors when the output is read, as the presence of double quotes within double-quoted strings is not valid.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (strchr(val, '"'))
    q = '\'';
else
    q = '"';
ret = snprintf(dst, rest(dst, end), "%c%s%c%s", q, val, q, vnode->next ? ", " : "\n");
```
```c
ret = snprintf(dst, rest(dst, end), "\"\"\n");
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a check for the presence of double quotes in the value. If a double quote is found, it uses single quotes to enclose the value instead. This ensures that values containing double quotes are correctly formatted, preventing parsing errors. The second statement ensures that an empty value is still handled correctly by outputting an empty string.

